P-W2, spec 001 W2: the switch-on, for the Author's hand.

Built from plain copies: infra/account/handover.tf and infra/prod/handover.tf as W1's thirteen patches leave them (a
git archive export of 0bf73883, W1's tip, with P-I3, P-G1, P-G3, P-I7, P-G4, P-G2, P-I4, P-G5, P-6, P-I5, P-G6, P-I6,
and P-G7 applied in that order); edited in $TMPDIR, then diff -u with a/ and b/ labels after this preamble; never git
apply, and nothing written into any worktree. Apply it on W2's -p branch, with `patch -p1 < <this file>` at the
repository's root, and commit the result byte for byte (tasks.md T175, T177).

What it holds (tasks.md T175; plan.md §16, "Why a switch" and "W2's order"):
  - both roots' local.wired = true: what W1 wrote behind the switch, `count = local.wired ? 1 : 0`, is made by W2's
    applies, and aws_ec2_instance_state.ec2_w1, behind `!local.wired`, is dropped, which leaves EC2 as it is;
  - nothing else: each switch sits in a locals block of its own, so the patch changes that block alone.

Its checks, on a fresh export with W1's thirteen patches and this one, in that order: infra/test's T046, T084, T104,
T119, and T159 checks pass with both switches on; on the same export without this patch,
test_prod_network.py::test_w2_holds_both_switches_on fails (the switches off).
